Jesus' family lineage
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah, in his forefathers, is divided into two sections, which are distinguished by the name of two persons among his forefathers. So Judah is the father of Abu 'Abd al-'Uzza and the father of Sayyarah. And
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salem was Boaz's father. Rahab was Boaz's mother. Boaz was Obed's father. Ruth was Obed's mother. Obed was Jesse's father.
Jesse was the father of King David. David was the father of Solomon. Solomon's mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa is the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat is the father of Jehoram. Jehoram is the father of Ahaziah.
Uzaziya was the father of Ya'qub. And Ya'qub was the father of Uziyya. And Uziyya was the father of Ahza'. And Ahza' was the father of Hezron.
Hashkakiyyah, the breakfaster was Mansuh. Mansuh was the breakfaster Amun. Amun was the breakfaster Yusha'iyah.
And Joshua the son of Nun was old and advanced in age; and he was living among the people. And in those days, the people of the Jewish nation were exiled to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jeroam was the father of Shallum. Shallum was the father of Zimri.
Zerubbabel is the father of Abiud. Abiud is the father of Eliezer. Eliezer is the father of Azariah.
Azur was the father of Zadu. Zadu was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Eliud is the father of Eliezer. Eliezer is the father of Matthan. Matthan is the father of Jacob.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. Joseph is the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
From Abraham, peace be upon him, to David, peace be upon him, there were fourteen generations. Then from David, peace be upon him, to the coming of the children of Israel to Babylon, there were fourteen generations. Then from the coming of the children of Israel to Babylon to the Messiah, there were fourteen generations. And Jesus, the Messiah, was born.
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they began to live together, it became clear that she would have a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a devout man. He did not wish to publicly shame her. Therefore, he planned to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary your wife into your home. For the child who is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She will have a child. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins."
All this has happened to lead to what the Lord said would happen. He said it through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us".
Joseph woke up. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have marital relations with her until after she gave birth to a son. And Joseph gave him the name Jesus.
The wise men visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in the land of Judah. This happened while Herod was king over the land of Judah. And after Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and everyone in Jerusalem was also disturbed.
And Herod called together all the chief priests and the rulers of the people from among the children of Israel. And he called for the wise men who were in
He said, "This is what the prophet wrote, and he said, '
"But a house for you, in the land of Judah. They are without doubt the least important among the cities of Judah. A ruler will come out from you. And he will be the shepherd of my people Israel."
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them the exact time when the star had appeared.
He sent them to a house they had. And he said, "Go! Then carefully and skillfully examine the soul for the newborn. For just by his presence, I will give him a statement. Then at that time I can go to worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they departed on their way. As for the star that they had seen when they were in the east, it went before them. And in the end, this star stopped above the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down before him and honored him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. The flight into Egypt of the family of Jesus
When the wise men had gone, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream. The angel said, "Get up! Take the child and his mother and escape to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you. Herod will search for the child to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They stayed there until King Herod died. In this way, the words of the Lord spoken by the prophet were fulfilled. He had said, "I have indeed brought my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became very angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were two years old or less were to be killed. This was in agreement with the time that the wise men had seen the star.
In this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, were fulfilled. For he said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, lamenting and great mourning. Raepha weeps for her lamentations, providing for her children. She refuses to be comforted, for her absence is at hand. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Joseph got up. He took the child and his mother Mary with him, returning to the land of Israel.
But then he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After being warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
He has this, in the city that is called Nazareth. Thus were the sayings of the prophets concerning the Messiah true. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said: "Repent from what you do! The House of the Sky is near."
John is the one the prophet Isaiah spoke about. He said, "A voice of one calling in the desert, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make his paths straight.' " (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people came out to him from Jerusalem and all of Judea, and they also came from all of the region of the Jordan.
When they acknowledged their error, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bear fruit that shows you have distanced yourself from your sins.
Do not think that you can say to yourself, "Abraham is our father." I tell you, God is able to raise up children for Abraham even from these stones.
The axe is already laid at the roots of the trees. All trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be hewn and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling upon you to turn to the Torah. But after me, one will come who is stronger than I am. And I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
His pitchfork is in his hand to clear the straw from his threshing floor. He will gather his wheat into the storeroom. But he will burn up the husks with fire that can't be put out." Jesus Is Baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. So why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is fitting for us to do this. It fulfills the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
The heavens opened just as the Lord Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment he saw heaven open and the Spirit of God come down on him like a dove.
And when the voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Yusuf is tested.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of abstaining from food, Jesus felt hungry.
Indeed, the temptation came to him. And he said, "If you are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. And he made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are God's children," he said, "throw yourselves down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you carefully. They will support you with their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered, saying, "It is written also, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you bow down and stand humbly, you would say to me: 'I will give you all of this,' he said that."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. And angels came and took care of him. Jesus begins to preach.
John had been imprisoned. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. This was beside the lake, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In that way, it has borne out what the prophet Isaiah said. He said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews dwell. Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
They will see a great light. For they are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine for them." (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time on, Jesus began to preach, saying, "Turn away from your sins!" He also said, "The kingdom of heaven is near." After this, Jesus chose the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There, he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother James (son of Zebedee). They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Hello. Follow me," said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Leaving there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. As they were preparing their nets, Jesus called to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of God's kingdom. He healed every disease and illness the people had.
His fame spread throughout Syria. People brought to him all who were sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pain. Others were possessed by demons. Some were shaking violently. Others were completely unable to move. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. He led a group of people from the region of Galilee, and from the areas known as the 10 Decapolis cities, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Other groups of people came from the regions of southern areas of the Jordan River.
Christ bestows blessings.
